You are supporting a process mining research prototype.
Use only the synthetic scenario information provided below.
Do not invent company-specific system names, product names, confidential logs, internal metrics, or real organizational details.
Return valid JSON only. Do not wrap it in Markdown.

Scenario ID: S05
Scenario title: Specification Document Review
Initial workflow description:
An engineer reviews a specification document to check whether new features are reflected correctly and whether there are typos or missing details.

Domain expert answers:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What event or condition triggers the engineer to begin reviewing a specification document — for example, a notification from a document management system, a new version being published, or a manual request?",
      "answer": "The process starts when the engineer gathers the specification document together with the accompanying list of feature updates. This collection step signals the beginning of the review cycle. The specific trigger mechanism — such as an automated alert or a manual handoff — is not specified in the available information."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "In which system or location does the specification document live, and how does the engineer access the version that needs to be reviewed?",
      "answer": "The document is stored in a central storage repository. The engineer retrieves the relevant version from there, along with the prior version for comparison purposes. Exact access procedures or authentication steps are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "Does the engineer follow a defined checklist or set of criteria when reviewing the document, and if so, what are the main items on that list?",
      "answer": "Yes, a structured checklist guides the review. The main criteria are: verifying that all feature updates are captured in the document, and confirming that wording, formatting, and editorial quality are consistent throughout. These two areas form the core of the evaluation criteria."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "What are the possible outcomes at the end of the review, and what action does the engineer take for each outcome — for example, approving the document, requesting changes, or escalating to someone else?",
      "answer": "Two key decision points exist. First, if feature coverage is incomplete, the author must revise the document; if complete, the process continues. Second, after revisions are submitted, they are either accepted and forwarded for final sign-off, or sent back for another round of corrections."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "How does the engineer record and communicate review findings — for instance, through inline comments in the document, a separate feedback form, a tracking ticket, or an email?",
      "answer": "The engineer logs observations and requested edits using a dedicated commenting tool, producing a structured list of needed changes. These are also captured in a change-tracking system so that each revision request can be monitored and resolved systematically."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "Is the review performed solely by one engineer, or do other roles participate, and is there a defined process if reviewers disagree or if no response is received within a certain timeframe?",
      "answer": "Multiple parties are involved: the engineer conducting the review, the person who authored the document, the individual who owns the relevant features, and a final approver who grants official sign-off. Procedures for handling disagreements or non-responses within set timeframes are not specified."
    }
  ]
}
Task: Incorporate the answers and produce an updated structured workflow representation plus automation opportunities.
Record unknown facts as remaining gaps, rather than asserting unsupported details.
Return JSON with this shape:
